<%!-- ============================================================
  Containers — the runner in a container. The honest core is
  namespace visibility (a runner acts on what its namespace can
  reach). The official image (ghcr.io/andrewdryga/emisar-runner,
  published per runner release by runner-release.yml) covers the
  container observation set; extension is FROM + tools + emisar
  pack install. State persistence: cloud.token_path + the dispatch log;
  external identity follows the hostname unless runner.id is pinned. EMISAR_GROUP /
  EMISAR_RUNNER_ID env overrides relabel without a config mount. Kubernetes
  (DaemonSet) and Nomad (system job) build on this page. Facts
  verified against runner/release/Dockerfile, install.sh, and
  runner/internal/config.
============================================================ --%>
<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="containers"
      updated="August 4, 2026"
      source_path="portal/apps/emisar_web/lib/emisar_web/controllers/marketing_html/docs_containers.html.heex"
      toc={[
        {"what-it-sees", "What a containerized runner sees"},
        {"official-image", "The official image"},
        {"building-blocks", "Identity and state"},
        {"extending", "Extend it for your packs"},
        {"sidecar", "A sidecar next to one app"},
        {"orchestrators", "One per node: Kubernetes and Nomad"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="containers">
        <:dek>
          The runner is a plain foreground process — it runs fine in a container. What changes
          isn't whether it runs; it's what it can see.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="docker host"
        copy_text="docker run -d --name emisar -v emisar-data:/var/lib/emisar -e EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY=emkey-enroll-… ghcr.io/andrewdryga/emisar-runner:X.Y.Z"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> docker run -d --name emisar \
    -v emisar-data:/var/lib/emisar \
    -e EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY=emkey-enroll-… \
    ghcr.io/andrewdryga/emisar-runner:X.Y.Z
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> docker logs -f emisar</.docs_code>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Egress-only">
        Nothing listens inbound — the container dials out to
        <code class="text-zinc-300">emisar.dev</code>
        like any other runner.
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="what-it-sees">What a containerized runner sees</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A runner can only act on what its own namespace can reach. On a VM or bare metal that's
        the whole machine — which is why the
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/host-install"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          host install
        </.link>
        is the default: most packs are host-oriented, and some literally require a systemd
        host. Put the same runner in a bare container and those packs now see the container —
        its processes, its filesystem, its network.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        It gives you the freedom to design the runner scope that fits your specific use case.
        A sidecar that can only see one app is a tightly scoped runner — the agent operating
        through it can't touch anything else. You just have to pick the shape that matches
        what you want the agent to operate: one app, a node, or every node.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="official-image">The official image</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Every runner release publishes
        <code class="font-mono">ghcr.io/andrewdryga/emisar-runner</code>
        for linux/amd64 and linux/arm64, tagged with the release version — replace
        <code class="font-mono">X.Y.Z</code>
        with the current one from the <.external_link
          href="https://github.com/andrewdryga/emisar/releases"
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >releases page</.external_link>. There is no <code class="font-mono">latest</code>
        tag: the image contents are part of the runner's capability boundary, so moving to a new
        version is a deliberate act, like trusting a pack.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The image carries the exact release-built runner binary and three packs matched to what a
        container can see — <code class="font-mono">fs-search</code>
        (its filesystem), <code class="font-mono">debugging</code>
        (its processes), and <code class="font-mono">network-tls</code>
        (its network) — with exactly the tools those packs call, nothing more. It runs as a
        non-root user (uid <code class="font-mono">65532</code>), connects to
        <code class="font-mono">emisar.dev</code>
        unless <code class="font-mono">EMISAR_URL</code>
        points at a self-hosted control plane, reads the enrollment key from <code class="font-mono">EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY</code>, and keeps its state under <code class="font-mono">/var/lib/emisar</code>. The baked config also caps admission at
        low-risk actions, so the default image is observation-only — the debugging pack's fix-it
        verbs stay refused until you mount a config that admits them. Each release's notes carry
        the image digest, and the image ships signed build provenance and an SBOM:
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="shell"
        copy_text="gh attestation verify oci://ghcr.io/andrewdryga/emisar-runner:X.Y.Z --owner andrewdryga"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> gh attestation verify oci://ghcr.io/andrewdryga/emisar-runner:X.Y.Z --owner andrewdryga</.docs_code>

      <.docs_h2 id="building-blocks">Identity and state</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        By default, the runner uses the container hostname as its identity. After the first connect,
        the per-runner token lands at <code class="font-mono">cloud.token_path</code>
        (<code class="font-mono">/var/lib/emisar/token.json</code>
        in the official image). Keep the hostname stable and
        <code class="font-mono">/var/lib/emisar</code>
        in a volume so restarts preserve the same runner and its durable dispatch state.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Two environment variables relabel a fleet without a config file.
        <code class="font-mono">EMISAR_GROUP</code>
        sets <code class="font-mono">runner.group</code>
        — the image bakes <code class="font-mono">containers</code>, and a group is a dispatch
        target ("every runner in group web"), so give each fleet its own instead of pooling
        unrelated fleets in the default. <code class="font-mono">EMISAR_RUNNER_ID</code>
        pins <code class="font-mono">runner.id</code>
        when the orchestrator changes hostnames across placements but the workload is still one
        logical runner. Everything else — labels, admission — changes by mounting your own file
        over <code class="font-mono">/etc/emisar/config.yaml</code>:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="config.yaml"><span class="text-sky-300">schema_version</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">1</span>
<span class="text-sky-300">runner</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">id</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"payments-sidecar"</span>
  <span class="text-sky-300">group</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"sidecars"</span>
<span class="text-sky-300">paths</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">data_dir</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"/var/lib/emisar"</span>
<span class="text-sky-300">events</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">jsonl_path</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"/var/log/emisar/events.jsonl"</span>
<span class="text-sky-300">cloud</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">enrollment_key_env</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY"</span>
  <span class="text-sky-300">token_path</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"/var/lib/emisar/token.json"</span></.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        One more thing before a fleet: the key from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/runners/install"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Connect a runner</a>
        is single-use —
        right for one host, wrong for a template that scales. For DaemonSets and system jobs,
        mint a
        <a
          href={~p"/app/runners/keys/new"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >reusable enrollment key</a>
        (optionally capped at a number of uses) and hand that to the template. See
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runners" <> "#enrollment-keys"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >enrollment keys</.link>
        for the lifecycle details.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="extending">Extend it for your packs</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A pack that talks to your stack needs its client in the image — actions run from the
        container, so a <code class="font-mono">postgres</code>
        pack needs <code class="font-mono">psql</code>
        where the runner is. Build on the official image: install the tools the pack calls, then
        bake the pack itself with <code class="font-mono">emisar pack install</code>
        (the runner binary is already in the image):
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Dockerfile">FROM ghcr.io/andrewdryga/emisar-runner:X.Y.Z
USER root
RUN apt-get update \
    && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends postgresql-client \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \
    && emisar pack install postgres=X.Y.Z --hash sha256:…
USER 65532</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Pin the pack version and content hash —
        <a
          href={~p"/app/packs"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Packs</a>
        shows both — so the
        image build is reproducible and a tampered registry copy is rejected. Keep the additions
        as narrow as the workload allows. Building your own image from scratch instead — a
        different distro, a hardened golden base? Install the release binary with <code class="font-mono text-brand-300">--no-service</code>: it skips init detection, the
        service user, and the systemd unit, and just puts the binary in place. Supervision belongs
        to whatever runs the container: <code class="font-mono">docker run --restart</code>, a
        Kubernetes restart policy, or the Nomad client.
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="shell"
        copy_text="curl -sSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh | sudo bash -s -- --no-service"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -sSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh | sudo bash -s -- --no-service</.docs_code>

      <.docs_h2 id="sidecar">A sidecar next to one app</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        When the thing you want the agent to operate <em>is</em> the app — read its logs,
        inspect its processes, poke its admin CLI — run the runner as a second container that joins
        the app container's PID, network, and (read-only) volumes. The runner then sees exactly that
        app and nothing else; policy, approvals, and audit work unchanged.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> docker run -d --restart unless-stopped \
    --name emisar-runner \
    --pid container:payments \
    --network container:payments \
    --volumes-from payments:ro \
    -e EMISAR_URL=https://emisar.dev \
    -e EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY=emkey-enroll-… \
    -v /etc/emisar/config.yaml:/etc/emisar/config.yaml:ro \
    -v emisar-runner-data:/var/lib/emisar \
    ghcr.io/andrewdryga/emisar-runner:X.Y.Z</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The official image already covers a sidecar's core job — reading the app's files,
        processes, and network state; the command above supplies an operator-owned config and a
        persistent token volume. When the app's own CLI belongs in the loop,
        <a
          href="#extending"
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >extend the image</a>
        with exactly that client.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="orchestrators">One per node: Kubernetes and Nomad</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        To operate the <em>nodes</em> instead of a single app, run one runner per node — a
        Kubernetes DaemonSet or a Nomad system job. Both reuse the mechanics above (an image, a
        persistent state volume) and a reusable enrollment key; each has its own worked manifest:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <.link navigate={~p"/docs/kubernetes"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Kubernetes</strong>
            </.link>
            — a DaemonSet whose pod spec is your blast-radius dial: <code class="font-mono">hostPID</code>/<code class="font-mono">hostNetwork</code>
            and read-only host mounts decide exactly what each node's runner can see.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <.link navigate={~p"/docs/nomad"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Nomad</strong>
            </.link>
            — a <code class="font-mono">system</code>
            job placing one instance on every client node, with the release binary fetched in an
            <code class="font-mono">artifact</code>
            stanza and the token and dispatch state persisted on a host volume.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
